# Testability changes in production code
Owner allows small production edits only to make code testable. Prefer a small prod seam over a fragile test-side hack.

## Allowed (behaviour-neutral)
- Connections/settings from env (DB/Redis/Kafka URLs, WEBHOOK_URL, secrets, SMTP host) instead of hard-coded values; defaults keep current behaviour.
- Timing knobs via env (retry/backoff, beat and cleanup intervals), current values as defaults.
- Move import-time side effects (Kafka connect, Sentry/OTel init, metrics registration) to lifespan/startup; inert without env.
- A Depends() or constructor-injected dependency where a seam is genuinely needed (default keeps current behaviour).
- Compose healthchecks; frontend accessible names / data-testid; production frontend build target.
- Remove Datadog.

## Not allowed
Behaviour changes, bug fixes, renames, moving files, restructuring, new features, formatting-only churn: refactoring phase, with these tests as the safety net.

## Procedure
1. One line: what is missing and why a test-side patch is worse (use a seam when the patch would cost >30 min).
2. Smallest possible change; default behaviour identical.
3. Run existing checks (service starts, /health, manual lab smoke).
4. Separate commit, prefix `testability:`; add one line to testing/KNOWN_ISSUES.md ("Testability changes": file, what, why).

## Fallback
If a prod change is not worth it: dependency_overrides, monkeypatch before import, mocker.patch(autospec=True), env vars, stubbing a module in sys.modules.
